WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.620
It's great to welcome to the program today,
Margo Finn, who teaches in the applied liberal

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:05.620
arts initiative,

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:11.839
the university of Michigan and wrote a book
titled discriminating taste, how class anxiety

00:00:11.839 --> 00:00:16.840
created the food revolution. Margo, so great
to have you on today. Thanks for having me.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:20.650
So this is super interesting to me, uh, for,
for a number of different reasons including

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:25.439
that because of my background, I sort of have
lived in a number of different food cultures

00:00:25.439 --> 00:00:30.890
and um, have, have read a lot about conspicuous
consumption and the different ways in which

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:37.810
sometimes people choose to, uh, display either
sort of cultural knowledge or, or, uh, in

00:00:37.810 --> 00:00:43.460
sometimes some cases monetary status. So let's
go back a little bit. At what point did there

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:50.140
start to be sort of multiple food cultures
that started to correlate with some class

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:51.830
elements of society as well?

00:00:51.830 --> 00:00:56.170
That's a great question. I think that there's
probably been a relationship between how people

00:00:56.170 --> 00:01:00.900
eat and their social class as long as there's
been social classes. Um, and perhaps even

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:04.570
before there were, you know, the kinds of
groupings that we were recognized as social

00:01:04.570 --> 00:01:09.250
classes. Today, food has always been associated
with power and hierarchy and how you express

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:14.530
your identity. Um, so it's, it's not like
there was a moment where all of a sudden people

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:18.670
started performing class through food. Food
has always been a way that we express who

00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:22.780
we are in relation to other people. So it's
always been available as that vocabulary to

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:25.100
express class. What are some

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:32.580
of the examples about a belief about food
that results from or connects to economic

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:33.970
anxieties or concerns?

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:40.890
Um, let, let me see if I can think of a contemporary
example. So one that I, I sometimes use is

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:45.810
the affinity for kale, which we tend to recognize
right now is something that's um, sort of

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:49.970
a symbol of the alternative food movement.
It's seen as something that's healthy and

00:01:49.970 --> 00:01:55.150
virtuous. Maybe not that delicious. Although
you've got people who will, you know, who

00:01:55.150 --> 00:01:58.500
will advertise that they've got a great massage,
kale salad or something like that. But the

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:03.590
way people are talking about kale, it's got
this weird symbolic power to like kale mean

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:08.311
something about you. And people are using
that, I think sometimes to express that they

00:02:08.311 --> 00:02:13.780
have this interest in health interest in sustainability,
but it's also going along with a certain food

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:17.980
culture that we do associate with the upperclass
right now. So to advertise the way people

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:21.703
do on tee shirts and shopping bags that you
know, like kale to the victors. There's one

00:02:21.703 --> 00:02:26.670
that you see in Michigan, um, is a way of
trying to express that you're a part of this

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:31.990
elite food community or that you have these
concerns that are true of the elite food community.

00:02:31.990 --> 00:02:35.920
That's interesting because, so there's this
idea, so kale is a good example in the sense

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:42.280
that you said that very clearly kale is associated
with certain economics, but it's not totally

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:45.560
clear that kale tastes that good. And there's
sort of a question Mark around whether it

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:53.170
actually does or doesn't taste good. Is there
a divide in terms of whether the sort of more,

00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:58.680
whatever we would call them, more elevated
foods economically, are actually better tasting

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:03.209
foods? And is it really just a matter of preference?
Can we even assess that in any kind of empirical

00:03:03.209 --> 00:03:04.209
way?

00:03:04.209 --> 00:03:09.060
I, I don't know if there's any such thing
as like an empirically good tasting food because

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:13.390
we, we eat food but we eat food with stories
and the stories that we tell food tell us

00:03:13.390 --> 00:03:18.000
whether or not. So we could say that yes,
there's maybe an innate preference for sweetness,

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.880
but you'll also get, and this is I think more
true among the upperclass a real sense that

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:28.319
too sweet is not good. It's infant tile or
it's or like genuinely disliked. I don't like

00:03:28.319 --> 00:03:32.720
super sweet things, right? Because I've been
either taught that I'm not supposed to or

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:37.129
I've acquired different tastes. So what I
think happens is that people get motivated

00:03:37.129 --> 00:03:40.700
to like different things or motivated to try
different things. So I'm not saying that like

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:45.490
rich people are pretending to like kale. I
think that they may have had more of an incentive

00:03:45.490 --> 00:03:50.191
to develop an affinity for it to try that
massage kale salad and find out that if you

00:03:50.191 --> 00:03:53.620
eat it enough times or if you think of it
in a certain way, yeah, you might really like

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:57.410
it. I don't think they're faking that they
like kale. I think that there are reasons

00:03:57.410 --> 00:04:01.190
that they like kale that are connected to
what it might say about them and not have

00:04:01.190 --> 00:04:05.030
anything to do with its health or what it
actually tastes.

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:10.470
I I, I mean I, I wonder how much of this has
to do with the preferences that are sort of

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:16.979
instilled at a young age in this sense, which
is that we know that the, the foods that are

00:04:16.979 --> 00:04:24.550
cheapest per calorie are often foods that
contain subsidized corn being one example.

00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:29.680
So when you look at the products that have,
for example, a lot of high fructose corn syrup

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:34.740
and are cheaper per calorie or some of these
other products, it seems as though if for

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:40.360
economic reasons at a young age, those are
the foods that one becomes accustomed to,

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:45.490
that that probably has a big role to play
later on in life. In terms of preferences,

00:04:45.490 --> 00:04:51.029
I think that there is a sense that people
have an affinity for, for, you know, the foods

00:04:51.029 --> 00:04:55.939
that they ate as kids that you get used to
certain tastes, but I'd be wary of oversimplifying

00:04:55.939 --> 00:04:59.330
that narrative. I think that that's actually
a story that we like to tell about poor people,

00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:03.050
right? They can only, they, they eat this
junky food cause they can only afford this

00:05:03.050 --> 00:05:07.860
junky food and they're making an economically
rational choice to get the cheap calories.

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:12.909
And then that's all they know how to like
and eat. And I don't buy any part of that

00:05:12.909 --> 00:05:17.520
narrative. Everybody eats junk food when people
of all social classes, particularly as kids.

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:22.270
Um, and that doesn't mean that people eat
that way. Their whole lives. Many people's

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:27.330
eating preferences transformed. So the rich
people who are eating kale right now as adults

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:31.490
mostly I don't think grew up eating kale,
his children, I don't think that they, um,

00:05:31.490 --> 00:05:36.289
were given their kale, you know, in the 1950s,
1970s whenever they grew up that that just

00:05:36.289 --> 00:05:39.249
wasn't a part of the food culture in the same
way. And certainly wasn't, there wasn't a

00:05:39.249 --> 00:05:43.759
sense in the 70s that we need to feed kids
kale to do this taste education that people

00:05:43.759 --> 00:05:47.590
will talk about now. And yet many of those
people grew into adults who really loved their

00:05:47.590 --> 00:05:52.289
massage kale salad. So people do change their
tastes over time. Right?

00:05:52.289 --> 00:05:57.430
I think you're definitely right. I guess the
question would be whether the tastes might

00:05:57.430 --> 00:06:02.949
change for more people, were they not may
be constrained by economic factors when maybe

00:06:02.949 --> 00:06:07.360
some people's preferences start changing,
but I guess it's, it, it could be some part

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:11.919
of all of these, uh, anecdotally or to some
degree, I, I, I guess I'm not sure. I'm thinking

00:06:11.919 --> 00:06:13.099
out loud here.

00:06:13.099 --> 00:06:16.479
Yeah, I think the one thing that's interesting
is because food does so many different things

00:06:16.479 --> 00:06:20.379
in taste, us do so many different things that
we can, we can say these things that have

00:06:20.379 --> 00:06:24.219
a certain truth to them, right? Like people
like familiar flavors. Oh, that's true. But

00:06:24.219 --> 00:06:28.250
people also like novel flavors, right? So
people seek foods that are comforting that

00:06:28.250 --> 00:06:32.649
they grew up with, but they also seek foods
that are adventurous and new to explore new

00:06:32.649 --> 00:06:36.779
things. So I think, you know, we can, so when
we tell these stories about why do people

00:06:36.779 --> 00:06:40.889
like the things they like, um, one thing that
we have to be careful about is falling into

00:06:40.889 --> 00:06:45.020
the, there's very easy narratives, but they
can also conflict it just like the idea that

00:06:45.020 --> 00:06:48.919
we're all eating better, we know so much more,
so much more information and on the other

00:06:48.919 --> 00:06:52.349
hand, Oh, we're eating junk and it's crap
and our food system has been terrible. Those,

00:06:52.349 --> 00:06:55.860
you know, those both can't be true. It can't
be, everything is getting better. We're learning

00:06:55.860 --> 00:07:00.240
more. And also somehow we're forgetting all
of our ancestral wisdom. Like those don't

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:01.409
both get to be true.

00:07:01.409 --> 00:07:05.770
Well, it could be economically stratified,
right? Where you, you see a larger and larger

00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:10.369
divide between, I mean, that's in a sense
what you're talking about when you talk about

00:07:10.369 --> 00:07:16.409
the appeal of kale to certain people, but
not everybody. We could simultaneously have

00:07:16.409 --> 00:07:22.099
more information about what's good to eat
and growing numbers of people who are suffering

00:07:22.099 --> 00:07:25.960
from obesity and food related illness because
of inequality.

00:07:25.960 --> 00:07:29.349
Some of the assumptions people make about
obesity and how it correlates with social

00:07:29.349 --> 00:07:33.939
class. It's really complicated. So it's true
that among white women, rich white women are

00:07:33.939 --> 00:07:39.550
much thinner than poor white women. But it's
not as true if you look across race divides

00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:45.429
and if you look across gender divide, so among
um, Latino men and black men, the wealthier

00:07:45.429 --> 00:07:51.659
you are, the more likely you are to be obese.
And it's, and the, I, I forget what the relationship

00:07:51.659 --> 00:07:56.690
is with black women. I believe that Richard
Black women are thinner, but it's maybe not

00:07:56.690 --> 00:08:00.429
statistically significant in any case. So
it's not the case that just poor people are

00:08:00.429 --> 00:08:04.499
fatter. There's some real differences in how
those demographics are changing and it's not

00:08:04.499 --> 00:08:09.709
even clear that people are getting so, so
much fatter. People have gotten fatter somewhat,

00:08:09.709 --> 00:08:14.309
um, in the last, you know, 40 to 60 years
depending on how you're counting. But it's

00:08:14.309 --> 00:08:19.749
not a huge amount. It's not everybody. And
it's certainly not just the poor. So is it

00:08:19.749 --> 00:08:22.599
that poor people are eating junk food and
getting better and better while rich people

00:08:22.599 --> 00:08:26.409
are learning more about food and eating better
and better? I don't actually think either

00:08:26.409 --> 00:08:30.029
of those things are true and when we look
at things like who eats fast food, it's everybody

00:08:30.029 --> 00:08:33.701
right across the income spectrum about everybody
eats about one fast food meal a week. It's

00:08:33.701 --> 00:08:37.260
not like it's the poor people eating fast
food and the rich people aren't.

00:08:37.260 --> 00:08:43.420
Where do specialty diets fit into this? And
that could be specialty diets that, I mean

00:08:43.420 --> 00:08:48.130
I guess we could categorize them a few different
ways. You would have some, whether they are

00:08:48.130 --> 00:08:53.580
for health reasons or for other reasons or
a mix kind of depends. But we're talking about

00:08:53.580 --> 00:08:59.440
everything from a veganism and vegetarianism,
gluten free, which is increasingly popular.

00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:05.171
Paleo, there are now some prominent people
who have tried the carnivore diet. Um, and

00:09:05.171 --> 00:09:12.050
different versions of that fruitarian ism,
ketogenic diets are those. My instinct is,

00:09:12.050 --> 00:09:15.900
and I'm glad that you're pushing back against
sometimes what seems obvious is not true.

00:09:15.900 --> 00:09:20.500
My instinct is that there is a certain degree
of privilege associated with some of those

00:09:20.500 --> 00:09:24.310
choices. Where does that fit into the conversation
that we're having?

00:09:24.310 --> 00:09:28.400
Well, I, I do think, I think you're right.
I think that we at least there's a cultural

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:32.460
sense that these are the kinds of diets that
it takes a kind of privilege to pursue. Many

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:35.890
of them involve, um, eating foods that are
more expensive, especially if you're talking

00:09:35.890 --> 00:09:40.240
about paleo, low-carb, those are the meat
centric diets. It's really hard to do that

00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:45.840
cheaply. So I think there you do have to have
a certain kind of economic privilege and also

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:50.800
maybe access to knowledge and more than that,
I think even access to the kinds of cultural

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:55.960
rewards. So I think some people are really
rewarded for trying new diets and for aspiring

00:09:55.960 --> 00:09:59.810
to different things where whether it's health
or ethics through their diet. And I think

00:09:59.810 --> 00:10:04.630
for some people the incentives and the rewards
aren't there, right? In some communities to

00:10:04.630 --> 00:10:07.030
eat vegan is not going to be rewarded.

00:10:07.030 --> 00:10:10.760
It's not going to be seen as an admirable
choice. It's going to be seen as weird or

00:10:10.760 --> 00:10:16.800
antisocial or fussy or picky or bizarre. Those
things aren't going to be, so you're, you're

00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:20.510
not going to be as likely to try if that's
the way your community is going to see it.

00:10:20.510 --> 00:10:23.380
And if that's the way people are going to
react to you as if people are going to react

00:10:23.380 --> 00:10:27.000
with like, Oh, you look great and Oh, that's
so good for you and Oh, I couldn't do that.

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:30.690
You know, like, just a different kind of reaction
that people get for those diets.

00:10:30.690 --> 00:10:35.710
So you, you mentioned the fast food element
that, that there are a wealthy people, middle-class

00:10:35.710 --> 00:10:39.840
people, poor people, and generally speaking,
the amount of fast food consumption seems

00:10:39.840 --> 00:10:48.610
to be similar. How do we, uh, explain the
positioning of fast food across the economic

00:10:48.610 --> 00:10:52.390
spectrum? Uh, when Donald Trump was running
for president, there was a lot made of these

00:10:52.390 --> 00:10:58.190
having buckets of KFC on his private plane.
He loves his taco bowls and he loves Hispanics.

00:10:58.190 --> 00:11:01.630
I mean, that was a whole other fiasco that
we could talk about. But in any case, the

00:11:01.630 --> 00:11:06.400
taco, regardless of his feeling about Hispanic
people, the Trump tower taco bowl was really

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:10.630
great. Where does the fast food element fit
in culturally and economically?

00:11:10.630 --> 00:11:15.770
I mean, I think fast food does have a different
meaning for people depending on where you're

00:11:15.770 --> 00:11:21.380
located. So it is true that when poorer people
eat out, they're more likely to eat at a fast

00:11:21.380 --> 00:11:25.380
food restaurant because full service restaurants
are more expensive, but they're not necessarily

00:11:25.380 --> 00:11:28.770
going to be eating fast food a whole lot because
fast food isn't that cheap, right? The average

00:11:28.770 --> 00:11:33.590
fast food meal costs about $7 and the average
meal cooked and eaten at home costs between

00:11:33.590 --> 00:11:38.080
two to $4. So it is true that poor and working
class people eat more meals cooked at home

00:11:38.080 --> 00:11:42.220
because they can't afford to eat up. Even
at fast food restaurants, if they do eat out,

00:11:42.220 --> 00:11:45.020
they're more likely to eat at a fast food
restaurant than a full service restaurant.

00:11:45.020 --> 00:11:49.940
Right? So Richard people have this option
of fast food restaurants and more elaborate

00:11:49.940 --> 00:11:53.260
restaurants. And when they choose fast food,
they're not choosing, you know, to go eat

00:11:53.260 --> 00:11:57.220
a meal out. They're choosing the quick convenient
thing. That's often true for poor people too.

00:11:57.220 --> 00:12:02.640
Whereas they have the option of the nicer
restaurant meal as well. Yes.

00:12:02.640 --> 00:12:07.890
I mean it was, so when I lived in New York
city, I would sometimes walk by all of garden

00:12:07.890 --> 00:12:14.380
and it was packed and all of garden in New
York city is not universally less expensive

00:12:14.380 --> 00:12:19.720
than an actual local Italian restaurant. And
I remember at the time really giving some

00:12:19.720 --> 00:12:25.710
thought to that and imagining, well maybe
there's tourists who know all of garden and

00:12:25.710 --> 00:12:30.090
they don't know what's a reliable local Italian
place. Like there has to be more than just

00:12:30.090 --> 00:12:35.060
the economics of it. When the price seems
to be relatively similar. I wonder what you

00:12:35.060 --> 00:12:36.060
would make of that.

00:12:36.060 --> 00:12:40.730
Well, yeah, there's all kinds of things going
on, right? Who thinks that the, um, holding

00:12:40.730 --> 00:12:45.430
the wall are family owned or authentic Italian
restaurant is definitely superior. Right?

00:12:45.430 --> 00:12:50.100
What, what class of people is going to think
that, um, that even if in fact sometimes a

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:54.270
lower price at a family restaurant is going
to signal to them authenticity and perhaps

00:12:54.270 --> 00:12:59.400
the promise of better tasting food then then
all of garden, which is a chain and because

00:12:59.400 --> 00:13:05.980
it's mass produced is sort of inherently barred
from having authentic credibility. So there's

00:13:05.980 --> 00:13:10.690
just, I do think that it's the story and what's
going to appeal to two different people in

00:13:10.690 --> 00:13:15.680
the story is familiarity. And maybe, I mean
a lot of people love all of garden, right?

00:13:15.680 --> 00:13:20.940
Like the free or the, the endless breadsticks
and the salad and the soup. It's good food

00:13:20.940 --> 00:13:22.180
and you know what you're going to get.

00:13:22.180 --> 00:13:25.460
And it's the same thing every time. There
are some people who that's going to appeal

00:13:25.460 --> 00:13:29.230
to. And I do think that there are class dynamics
to that too. Whether or not you value familiarity

00:13:29.230 --> 00:13:33.470
and reliability and something that's going
to be pleasing to you and your whole family,

00:13:33.470 --> 00:13:38.360
or whether or not you value having an adventure,
trying something new, doing something that

00:13:38.360 --> 00:13:42.501
might be authentic and exotic that might expose
you to some other kinds of people that you

00:13:42.501 --> 00:13:47.230
then can return to your safe life. But you
had a, you know, some adventurous experience.

00:13:47.230 --> 00:13:50.720
These are in some ways maybe their personality
things, right? It's not that there aren't

00:13:50.720 --> 00:13:55.600
people who are adventurous eaters, even if
they have extremely, um, you know, limited

00:13:55.600 --> 00:13:59.590
means, but it does mean that there's just
different rewards that are going to motivate

00:13:59.590 --> 00:14:02.770
different people. And it's not clear to me
that it's the rich people doing all the good

00:14:02.770 --> 00:14:07.060
things and the poor people doing terrible
things. Are they thinking different stories?

00:14:07.060 --> 00:14:12.740
Our diets within the same culture right now
obviously if we can compare the average Japanese

00:14:12.740 --> 00:14:18.290
diet to the average Kenyan diet, they will
be very, very different. But within countries,

00:14:18.290 --> 00:14:25.900
within cultures, is the range of diets way
more disparate now than it was in times past?

00:14:25.900 --> 00:14:28.790
Or has or, or is that not necessarily the
case?

00:14:28.790 --> 00:14:33.670
That's a, that's a good question. It's a big
question. Um, I think it depends on what you're

00:14:33.670 --> 00:14:39.470
talking about, right? Like there's been military
conquest and globalization, global encounters

00:14:39.470 --> 00:14:44.690
that have changed how people eat for a long
time and whether there's homogeneity within

00:14:44.690 --> 00:14:49.250
countries or empires, depends on the moment.
It depends on how many people are being grouped

00:14:49.250 --> 00:14:53.980
together, how big the country is. Um, I think
you, you know, with the, with the more rapid

00:14:53.980 --> 00:14:58.790
movement of people in foods and ideas, you
may get more diversity within some places,

00:14:58.790 --> 00:15:02.820
but on the other hand you'll hear people talking
about the industrialization of food and maybe

00:15:02.820 --> 00:15:07.610
that's sort of flattened out the way some
food cultures look or, or made things more

00:15:07.610 --> 00:15:12.520
homogenous. That's, um, there's, there's kind
of so many moving parts. It's a hard question

00:15:12.520 --> 00:15:13.520
to answer. It's a good question.

00:15:13.520 --> 00:15:18.870
Oh, we've been speaking with Margo fin who
teaches in the applied liberal arts initiative

00:15:18.870 --> 00:15:24.110
at the university of Michigan. The book is
called discriminating taste, how class anxiety

00:15:24.110 --> 00:15:26.279
created the food revolution.

00:15:26.279 --> 00:15:31.250
Margot, really appreciate your time and super
interesting stuff. Thanks so much for having

00:15:31.250 --> 00:15:31.740
me.

